<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Djibouti</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Djibouti</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Djibouti is a country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, with a coast on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aden">Gulf of Aden</a>. In the late 19th century the colony of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a> was established after the ruling Dir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> sultans signed treaties with the French. Its railroad to Dire Dawa and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> let its port quickly supersede <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a> as the outlet for southern Ethiopia. The territory was renamed the French Territory of the Afars and the Issas in 1967, and a decade later the Djiboutian people voted for independence, founding the Republic of Djibouti, named after its capital. Armed conflict in the early 1990s ended in a power-sharing agreement in 2000.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Djibouti has an area of 23,200 square kilometres. It is bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> to the south, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> to the southwest and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> to the north, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aden">Gulf of Aden</a> to the east. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bab-el-mandeb">Bab-el-Mandeb</a> region has often been considered a primary crossing point for early hominins on a southern coastal route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> to South and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. It is named for its capital, the City of Djibouti, and the origin of the name is disputed: one theory derives it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> word gabouti, meaning plate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Djibouti area has been inhabited since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. In antiquity the territory was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Land of Punt</a>, which ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> reached by expeditions beginning, on the earliest record, with one organised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahure">Sahure</a> of the Fifth Dynasty in the 25th century BCE. Zeila, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>, was the capital of Adal, established by local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> clans in the early 9th century, and one of the earliest cities to embrace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The collapse of Adal left rump states including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aussa">Aussa</a>, Tadjourah and Rahayto.</p><p>The present boundaries were established as the first French foothold in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scramble-for-africa">Scramble for Africa</a>. On 11 March 1862 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> sultan Raieta Dini Ahmet signed a treaty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> selling the lands around Obock. The French wanted a coaling station for steamships, which became more important after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> opened in 1869. On 26 March 1885 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/issa-clan">Issas</a> became a French protectorate. Referendums on independence in 1958 and 1967 were rejected. A third, held on 8 May 1977, was backed by 98.8 percent of the electorate, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-gouled-aptidon">Hassan Gouled Aptidon</a>, an Issa politician, became the first president.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Djibouti is a unitary presidential republic of six regions, one of which is the City of Djibouti. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 20 May 2024 counted 1,066,809 people, the smallest population in mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. French and Arabic are the official languages, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> are national languages, and about 94 percent of Djiboutians are Muslim. The Somalis and the Afar are the two largest ethnic groups. The port serves as the principal outlet for imports to and exports from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, the country hosts foreign military bases, and the Intergovernmental Authority on Development has its headquarters in Djibouti City.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Djibouti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Somaliland has nevertheless built informal ties abroad, with representative offices in several countries, mutual representative offices with Taiwan opened in 2020, and close links with Ethiopia, for which Berbera offers an alternative to the port of <b>Djibouti</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-somaliland">Republic of Somaliland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
